I just finished reading Miyamoto Musashi: "The book of five rings." 
Musashi was a famous undefeated swordsman/samurai in 17th century Japan. 
In this book he teaches his five principles in the course of five 
scrolls, the scroll of earth, the scroll of water, the scroll of fire, 
the scroll of wind and the scroll of emptiness. Musashi was a real man, 
not some D&D fantasy, his art is directly relevant to the game of 
Nethack -- to commemorate him in the game is the least we can do.

There are just too many coinciding factors to be ignored. In Nethack we 
have samurais, we have japanese weapons and armor, we have martial arts, 
we face enemies in deadly combat, we have scrolls of earth, we have 
scrolls of fire, we have enlightenment. We have _planes_ of earth, air, 
fire and water, we just lack a few scrolls. It strikes me as especially 
fitting that a Samurai should be able to read the scrolls of earth, 
fire, water, wind and emptiness, and become enlightened. (Perhaps Monks 
and Priests as well, perhaps everyone)

We need to invent scrolls of water, wind and emptiness, of course. The 
scroll of water could create pools like an overflowing fountain (would 
evaporate in hell). The scroll of wind could create 3-6 random vortices 
and air elementals according to the level of the player, independently 
tame or hostile according to blessed or cursed state of the scroll in 
the same manner as a figurine. The scroll of emptiness... no need to 
invent, here is the clever part:

True rumor:
"It is said that a scroll of amnesia is like a scroll of emptiness."

Oracle consultation (perhaps applicable only to qualified characters):
"The famous samurai Musashi taught his principles of success in combat 
in the scrolls of earth, water, fire and wind. These scrolls must be 
studied carefully. However, true enlightenment can only come by finally 
reading the scroll of emptiness."
((Deliberately obscure reference to "scroll of emptiness"))

So, to achieve the goal, you must have read (not just identified) all 
the four "elemental" scrolls first. With a certain apprehension, you 
decide to deliberately empty your mind by reading the scroll of amnesia:

"Reading the scroll your mind is filled with emptiness." --More--
"You attain the sublime state of Enlightenment!" --More--
... normal enlightenment effect, among others:
"You are Enlightened."

There should be at least one more desirable (and predictable!) effect to 
make the process worthwhile. Perhaps gain an intrinsic like telepathy, 
warning, searching or some other skill normal for an accomplished 
martial artist but otherwise difficult to obtain (esp for 
Samurais/Monks/Priests) (at least this could help vegetarian conduct 
players). Perhaps bring the luck to maximum, perhaps something else, 
levitation like a blessed scroll, a moment of invulnerability, full 
healing, gain energy, gain level.

 From this point, reading a scroll of amnesia always gives the 
enlightenment effect. The scrolls of amnesia are useless anyway once 
they have been identified, now they will have a positive function. 
However, if your mind is sucked and you forget one of the scrolls of 
earth, water, fire or wind, the scroll of amnesia would work as usual, 
*haha*.


